# Note writing

Write evidence notes using a STE-inspired clarity discipline, adapted to explanatory research prose rather than aircraft procedures.
Use short, complete sentences. Give each sentence one main claim; state causal and conditional connections explicitly.
Use an explicit subject and active voice when the source identifies the actor. Keep passive voice when the actor is unknown.
Use one consistent term for each entity, operation, metric, and representation. Preserve exact names, symbols, numbers, units, and versions.
Write each Cue Note as an evergreen note in the Zettelkasten tradition: atomic, self-contained, and titled by its claim.
Write each Cue as one complete declarative sentence that states the main claim its Note supports. A reader who sees only the Cue learns the finding and can decide whether to open the Note. Keep the scope qualifier that makes the claim true. Do not use a topic label or an exam-keyword pile.
Give each Cue Note one idea. Split a Cue Note that carries two independent claims. Keep a claim together with the mechanism, conditions and limits that qualify it.
Make each Cue and each Note readable without its Section or neighboring Notes. Name the specific system, component, dataset or party by its proper name. Do not let "the authors", "the project", "this model" or a pronoun stand for a name that the Note has not stated.
Organize each Note as a self-contained explanation: its supported claim, necessary mechanism or observations, and applicable conditions or limits.
Use as many short sentences or short paragraphs as needed to preserve that explanation. Do not compress multiple mechanisms into a long sentence to meet a 1-3-sentence quota. Do not add padding.
For a procedure or comparison, use a short list only when it makes the sequence or distinct conditions clearer.
Preserve every material scope qualifier and uncertainty. Do not turn a proposal into a finding, correlation into causation, or an unverified release into an open model.
Do not invent causes, explanations, evidence, or conclusions to improve readability. Keep necessary technical detail and evidence provenance.
These are writing rules, not permission to change source selection, factual scope, evidence verification, or output language. Do not claim formal ASD-STE100 compliance.
